--- body ---
## Context

`scripts/guard-commit-on-main.sh` is deterministic enforcing behavior, not advice. Porting
this one guard tests the hardest boundary: whether Claude Code, Codex and Pi adapters can
intercept an action, normalize enough input and preserve a blocking decision. This ticket
does not create a general lifecycle schema, capability resolver or full policy catalog.

## Relevant files

- `scripts/guard-commit-on-main.sh` — canonical deterministic guard
- `settings.shared.json` — existing Claude wiring
- `rules/git.md` — normative commit/worktree policy
- tests for `guard-commit-on-main.sh` — current behavior contract

## Actions

1. Record the current allowed/blocked cases, payload fields, exit semantics and cwd-less gap.
2. Close fail-open parsing before using the guard as a reference: missing or untrusted `cwd`,
   `git -C`, global Git options, `env git`, aliases/wrappers, nested shells and compound
   commands must resolve the actual target repository or fail closed before mutation.
3. Keep the shell guard canonical; add only thin input normalization in each adapter.
4. Wire it through native pre-action enforcement in Claude Code, Codex and Pi where the
   harness provides a blocking mechanism.
5. Define refusal at the adapter-profile boundary: if Git-mutating shell access is enabled
   without reliable pre-action blocking, the enforcing profile cannot activate. Read-only
   slices may remain available only in an explicitly named reduced profile that makes no IDH
   safety claim; instructions or post-hoc warnings are not enforcement.
6. Add adversarial adapter tests that remove or weaken the guard wiring and must fail.
7. Add a canary value and prove neither normalization nor diagnostics leak it.
8. Extend `adapters/pilot-support.json` with tested enforcement and refusal assertions.

## Test

- Replay allowed, main-branch, primary-checkout, malformed and cwd-less fixtures through all
  three adapters, including `git -C`, Git options, wrappers and compound commands. Mutation-
  test removal of the event mapping, block result and exit semantics independently.

## Verification

- [ ] Existing safe Claude cases remain unchanged; recorded bypass, malformed and cwd-less
      cases now fail closed
- [ ] Codex and Pi either block the same forbidden cases or refuse activation explicitly
- [ ] No Git-mutating adapter profile activates without the required enforcing boundary
- [ ] Adapter weakening and missing-hook positive controls fail distinctly
- [ ] Canary scans cover arguments, diagnostics and logs
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- Portability cannot turn enforcement into advice.
- The guard owns the decision; adapters only normalize input and carry the decision.
- An unsupported adapter is safer than a silently weakened one.
- Stopping after this ticket leaves existing Claude enforcement intact.

## Exit criteria

- [x] Round-2 panel cleared the `needs-human` gate
- [ ] One canonical guard is exercised through Claude Code, Codex and Pi
- [ ] Unsupported enforcement paths fail closed and appear in the slice inventory
- [ ] No generalized policy catalog, lifecycle IR or capability resolver was introduced
